{
  "title": "How Freelance Designers Can Avoid Burnout",
  "seriesTag": "Designer Wellness",
  "brand": {
    "apply": false,
    "paper": "#EFE9DA",
    "ink": "#212B21",
    "accent": "#C08A28",
    "headline": "editorial",
    "body": "clean"
  },
  "slides": [
    {
      "title": "Burning Out Is Not a Badge of Honor",
      "body": "Freelance design is exciting — until it isn't. Burnout is real, it's common, and it's preventable. Here's how to protect your energy before you hit empty.",
      "isCover": true
    },
    {
      "title": "Set Working Hours and Actually Keep Them",
      "body": "Without a 9-to-5 structure, work bleeds into everything. Define your start and end times, block them on your calendar, and treat them like client meetings you can't cancel.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Stop Saying Yes to Every Project",
      "body": "Overcommitting is the fastest road to exhaustion. Before accepting new work, ask: Does this fit my capacity, my rates, and my creative interests? If not, it's okay to pass.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Build Breaks Into Your Workflow",
      "body": "Schedule short breaks between tasks and real days off every week. Resting isn't lost time — it's what keeps your creative thinking sharp and your output quality high.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Separate Your Identity From Your Output",
      "body": "Your worth is not your latest project. Bad feedback, slow months, and creative blocks don't define you. Build a life outside of design so one bad week doesn't feel like total failure.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Raise Your Rates Before You Resent Your Clients",
      "body": "Undercharging forces you to overwork just to cover the basics. Revisit your pricing regularly. Fair rates let you take on fewer projects, do better work, and actually enjoy the process.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Save This Before Your Next Deadline Spiral",
      "body": "Bookmark this carousel and come back to it when the pressure builds. Share it with a freelance friend who needs the reminder too.",
      "isCover": true
    }
  ]
}

{
  "title": "Cash-Flow Forecasting for First-Time Agency Owners",
  "seriesTag": "Agency Finance 101",
  "brand": {
    "apply": false,
    "paper": "#EFE9DA",
    "ink": "#212B21",
    "accent": "#C08A28",
    "headline": "editorial",
    "body": "clean"
  },
  "slides": [
    {
      "title": "Your agency looks profitable — and you still can't make payroll.",
      "body": "Revenue and cash are not the same thing. Forecasting fixes the gap before it becomes a crisis.",
      "isCover": true
    },
    {
      "title": "Start with when money actually arrives, not when you invoice.",
      "body": "A $10k project invoiced on the 1st might not clear until the 28th. Your forecast must reflect payment dates, not invoice dates.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Map your fixed outflows first.",
      "body": "Contractor retainers, software subscriptions, payroll — list every recurring cost and the exact date it hits your account each month.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Then layer in your variable costs.",
      "body": "Freelance support, ad spend, travel. These shift project to project. Flag which ones scale with revenue so you can see risk quickly.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "The 13-week rolling window is your most useful tool.",
      "body": "Forget annual projections for now. A 13-week view lets you spot a cash crunch 30–60 days before it lands — when you still have options.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Retainers smooth the forecast. Project work creates spikes.",
      "body": "If most of your revenue is project-based, build a cash buffer equal to at least one month of fixed costs before taking on new hires.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Save this and build your first 13-week forecast this week.",
      "body": "Use a spreadsheet. One row per week. Columns for expected inflows, fixed outflows, and variable outflows. Update it every Monday.",
      "isCover": true
    }
  ]
}

{
  "title": "Why Motivation Is Not Enough",
  "seriesTag": "Habit Myths Busted",
  "brand": {
    "apply": false,
    "paper": "#EFE9DA",
    "ink": "#212B21",
    "accent": "#C08A28",
    "headline": "editorial",
    "body": "clean"
  },
  "slides": [
    {
      "title": "You Don't Have a Motivation Problem",
      "body": "If you've ever fallen off a habit, you've probably blamed yourself for losing motivation. But motivation was never the right tool for the job.",
      "isCover": true
    },
    {
      "title": "Myth #1: Motivated People Are Consistent",
      "body": "Even the most disciplined people feel unmotivated regularly. Consistency isn't about feeling ready — it's about showing up when you don't.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Myth #2: If It Matters, You'll Stay Motivated",
      "body": "Caring deeply about a goal doesn't sustain daily action. Emotion fades. Structure doesn't.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Myth #3: You Just Need a Big Enough Why",
      "body": "A powerful reason helps you start. It rarely helps you continue. Habits need systems, not just meaning.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "What Actually Drives Consistency",
      "body": "Routine, environment design, and low-friction triggers do more work than willpower ever will. Build the conditions, not the mood.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "The Real Formula",
      "body": "Tiny habit + reliable cue + removed friction = behavior that doesn't depend on how you feel that morning.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Save This & Redesign Your Habits",
      "body": "Stop waiting to feel motivated. Start building the environment that makes motivation irrelevant. Save this post and revisit it when you feel stuck.",
      "isCover": true
    }
  ]
}

{
  "title": "Product Positioning",
  "seriesTag": "Marketing Fundamentals",
  "brand": {
    "apply": false,
    "paper": "#EFE9DA",
    "ink": "#212B21",
    "accent": "#C08A28",
    "headline": "editorial",
    "body": "clean"
  },
  "slides": [
    {
      "title": "Why Most Products Fail Before Launch",
      "body": "It's rarely the product. It's the position. If customers can't instantly understand who it's for and why it matters, they move on.",
      "isCover": true
    },
    {
      "title": "Positioning Is Not Your Tagline",
      "body": "Positioning is the strategic foundation beneath your messaging. Your tagline is the surface. Positioning is what makes it true.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "The 3 Questions Every Position Must Answer",
      "body": "Who is it for? What does it do better than alternatives? Why should they believe you? Answer all three — or customers will answer for you, usually wrong.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Own a Category, Not Just a Feature",
      "body": "Features get copied. Categories get owned. Position your product as the definitive solution to a specific problem, not a list of capabilities.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Your Competitor Set Shapes Perception",
      "body": "What you place your product next to determines how it's valued. Compared to agencies, your SaaS looks affordable. Compared to free tools, it looks premium. Choose your frame deliberately.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Weak Positioning Sounds Like Everyone Else",
      "body": "'Fast, easy, and powerful' is not a position — it's filler. Strong positioning is specific enough to exclude some customers and magnetic to the right ones.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Save This and Reposition with Clarity",
      "body": "Come back to this when your messaging stops converting. Revisit the three questions, audit your competitor frame, and sharpen who you're really for.",
      "isCover": true
    }
  ]
}

{
  "title": "Product Positioning: Own Your Market",
  "seriesTag": "Marketing Fundamentals",
  "brand": {
    "apply": true,
    "paper": "#000000",
    "ink": "#FFFFFF",
    "accent": "#E0000E",
    "headline": "editorial",
    "body": "clean"
  },
  "slides": [
    {
      "title": "Why Most Products Get Ignored",
      "body": "It's not a quality problem. It's a positioning problem. If customers can't instantly understand why you're different, they move on.",
      "isCover": true
    },
    {
      "title": "Positioning Is Not Your Tagline",
      "body": "Positioning is the mental space you own in your customer's mind. It's the answer to: 'Why you, over everyone else?'",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "The 3 Core Elements",
      "body": "1. Who it's for. 2. What problem it solves. 3. Why only you solve it this way. If any one is fuzzy, your positioning fails.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Category Design Wins",
      "body": "The strongest brands don't fight for a category — they create one. Define the game so you're the only logical winner.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Your Enemies Are Alternatives, Not Competitors",
      "body": "Customers compare you to doing nothing, using spreadsheets, or calling a friend. Position against the real alternative — not just rivals.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Test Your Positioning in One Sentence",
      "body": "Fill in the blank: 'We help [WHO] do [WHAT] without [PAIN].' If you can't, your positioning isn't clear yet.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Save This & Sharpen Your Position Today",
      "body": "Revisit your positioning statement this week. Apply the one-sentence test and close the gaps before your next campaign launches.",
      "isCover": true
    }
  ]
}

{
  "title": "Product Positioning for Early-Stage Founders",
  "seriesTag": "Founder Fundamentals",
  "brand": {
    "apply": false,
    "paper": "#EFE9DA",
    "ink": "#212B21",
    "accent": "#C08A28",
    "headline": "editorial",
    "body": "clean"
  },
  "slides": [
    {
      "title": "Nobody buys your product. They buy the version of themselves it creates.",
      "body": "That's the core idea behind product positioning — and most early-stage founders get it wrong from day one.",
      "isCover": true
    },
    {
      "title": "Positioning is not your tagline.",
      "body": "It's the internal logic that explains who your product is for, what it does, and why it beats every alternative. Your tagline comes after.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Start with the problem, not the product.",
      "body": "Before writing a single word of copy, name the specific pain your customer feels right now — not the general category you compete in.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "'Best alternative' beats 'no alternative.'",
      "body": "Customers always compare you to something — a workaround, a competitor, doing nothing. Define what you win against and make that contrast explicit.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Your positioning will be wrong. That's fine.",
      "body": "Early positioning is a hypothesis. Talk to 10 customers this week, find the words they use to describe their pain, and steal those words directly.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "One audience. One problem. One promise.",
      "body": "Founders resist narrowing down because it feels like leaving money on the table. Focused positioning actually accelerates growth — broad positioning stalls it.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Save this and revisit your positioning every 90 days.",
      "body": "Markets shift, customers evolve, and your product grows. Strong positioning isn't set once — it's maintained. Tap save so this is here when you need it.",
      "isCover": true
    }
  ]
}

{
  "title": "Weak Signal or Real Evidence? A PM's Field Test",
  "seriesTag": "Product Discovery",
  "brand": {
    "apply": false,
    "paper": "#EFE9DA",
    "ink": "#212B21",
    "accent": "#C08A28",
    "headline": "editorial",
    "body": "clean"
  },
  "slides": [
    {
      "title": "That customer complaint might mean nothing — or everything. Here's how to tell.",
      "body": "Experienced PMs still get this wrong. One loud user, one support ticket, one Slack message from sales — and suddenly it's on the roadmap. This week, learn to separate noise from signal before it costs you a sprint.",
      "isCover": true
    },
    {
      "title": "A signal is not evidence. Know the difference.",
      "body": "A signal is a data point: one user said it, one metric dipped, one complaint landed. Evidence is a pattern: multiple unconnected sources saying the same thing, unprompted, in the same context. Don't promote a signal until it's earned that status.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Test 1 — Did they bring it up, or did you ask?",
      "body": "Prompted feedback inflates frequency. If you asked \"does X frustrate you?\", the answer is almost always yes. Unprompted mentions — in support logs, exit interviews, or unsolicited emails — carry far more weight. Filter your sources accordingly.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Test 2 — Can you name three users with the same specific problem?",
      "body": "Not three users who nodded along. Three users who described the same friction, in similar words, without being led there. If you can't name them and quote them, you don't have repeatable evidence yet.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Test 3 — Does the problem survive a follow-up conversation?",
      "body": "Weak signals collapse under scrutiny. Book a 15-minute follow-up with your strongest example. Ask: how often does this happen, what do you do instead, and what would you give up to fix it? Vague answers reveal a weak signal.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "The quick evidence scorecard PMs actually use.",
      "body": "Before escalating any signal, score it: (1) Three or more independent sources? (2) Unprompted mentions in at least two channels? (3) Survives a follow-up question? (4) Tied to a behavior, not just an opinion? Three or more yes answers = worth investigating. Fewer = watch list only.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Pick one open signal on your roadmap and run the scorecard on it this week.",
      "body": "Choose a feature or problem that made it onto your list based on customer input. Apply the four-question scorecard. If it scores below three, move it to the watch list and write one sentence explaining why. Share the result with your team — the conversation alone is worth it.",
      "isCover": true
    }
  ]
}
